    {
      "filename": "Portlaoise Abbeyleix Road Roadsign.JPG",
      "title": "File:Portlaoise Abbeyleix Road Roadsign.JPG",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/20/Portlaoise_Abbeyleix_Road_Roadsign.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portlaoise_Abbeyleix_Road_Roadsign.JPG",
      "width": 2592,
      "height": 1944,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "Public domain",
      "artist": "Sarah777",
      "description": "Typical Irish roadsign at roundabout junction in Portlaoise. Explanation of symbols/colours:\nThe background of the sign, in this case white (Regional Road (Rxxx)/local road (Lxxxx)), is based on the colour code for the road the sign/motorist is on.\nThe black-on-white diagram represents the roundabout ahead and the roads radiating from it.\nThe long black line indicates the direction of approach.\nIn this case the first exit from the roundabout is a local (unsignposted) road to the left.\nthe second exit is the N80 leading to Tullamore and access to the N7 west. This area is \"patched\" green as the N80 and N7 are both National Routes.\nthe third exit is the N80 leading to Carlow and Access to the N7 east. This area is also \"patched\" green as the N80 and N7 are both National Routes.\nthe gap in the \"roundabout symbol\" is to indicate that the next road is the one you are already on. You could use the roundabout to do a U-turn, provided you are not on a one-way street!\nOn the N80, heading for Tullamore and also on a section of the N7 heading west there there are low bridges. This is highlighted by typical black-on-yellow diamonds and the heights (in feet and in meters) are given alongside the yellow warning diamonds.\nAt the bottom right is a sign for a car parking facility.\nThis picture has been taken in Portlaoise at the Abbeyleix Road south of the Abbeyleix Road Roundabout.",
      "dateCreated": "2006-08-31",
